{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792780176,"nonce":"QCoNPzbAY3vxTh/7oVy2UaBd8JQ=","title":"Compiler: a return drops later labels in the same statement list","message":"TypedControlFlowGraph::split_at_control_flow returns as soon as it meets TypedStatement::Return, so every statement after it in that list is discarded, including a TypedStatement::Label that a TypedStatement::Goto elsewhere targets. The goto then jumps to a block that is never built and execution fails with \"unresolved block HirId(..)\". Lowering [Goto(L) on one path, Return(x), Label(L), Return(y)] in one list shows it. The TypeScript frontend nests each switch clause, and a loop body that gets a continue label, in a TypedStatement::Block, which contains the early return, so it does not need a fix; any frontend that places labels after a return in one list (the Goto and Label pattern Lua also uses) would hit it. A fix would end the block at the return and continue with a fresh unreachable block, as the Goto arm already does.","files":null}]}